    "patchSets": [
        {
            "number": 1,
            "revision": "8060b3625899f495b21acffd7a215503b09f7b58",
            "parents": [
                "9f8939e17b23cbdf30b1cb789ebc6139e9ae71a0"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/94/64694/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "createdOn": 1688677875,
            "author": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "I feel this goes against what we have been doing in bmcweb with mostly avoiding auto.. Do you feel strongly we want this? this is enforced by clang-tidy and the impact to the code here is low so I could be convinced but it feels counter to other feedack."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't know when I've ever really enforced that we need to use auto on BOTH sides of an argument.  The thing I generally comment on is some variation of\n\nauto foo = myOpaqueMethodCall();\n\nWhere it's not obvious in that context what the return type is, and more importantly, if myOpaqueMethodCall() changes from say uint64_t, to int64_t, there's no change to the call site that would make us check if that transform was safe.\n\nThis is only really changing stuff like\n\nFoo foo = static_cast<Foo>(myFoo);\n\nWhich, auto seems perfrectly fine.  Foo is already declared on the line, and there's no way that static_cast will ever change to return Bar, without that line changing."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "Still slightly concerned we might end up with more auto here because this change:\nauto foo = myOpaqueMethodCall();\n\nbut \ud83e\udd37, this probably better matches other OpenBMC repos, clang-tidy enforces, I don't have much of an opinion"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "Maybe leave open for a few more days, see if anyone else cares either way"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Yeah, I have the same worry.  \"Don't use auto\" is a lot easier advice to tell people than \"Only use auto where appropriate\"."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "I am still in the \"Don't use auto\" is a lot easier to tell people than \"only use auto in these cases\".. But \ud83e\udd37, in 2 years, my opinion is about the same.. If you want this, I am good."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Other option is we could apply these changes and just not enforce?  I do really wish there was a readability for checking for.\n\nauto x = foo();\n\nIdk, I'm on the fence here two, which probably means I should just drop it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "I think if we decide to go with these changes, we should just enforce them but I don't care much. I am fine with doing nothing here (my slightest preference but I don't care much), changing and enforcing (my 2nd), and then just changing."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vernon Mauery",
                        "email": "vernon.mauery@gmail.com",
                        "username": "vmauery"
                    },
                    "message": "I agree that this is one of those places where auto is welcome. Having the type twice in the line is verbose, and in all of these instances, the type is very clear based on the casted type."
                }
            ],
